\section*{Executive Summary}

SentinelScan identified a \textbf{ {{ risk.rating | latex_escape }} } overall risk posture for the assessed target. The scan recorded \textbf{ {{ risk.total_findings | latex_escape }} } findings across header security, transport security, form handling, and information disclosure. The most significant issues are the absence of essential browser security headers, a login form that submits over HTTP, and the lack of HTTPS on the target application.

For non-technical stakeholders, the practical concern is straightforward: sensitive information may be exposed in transit, the application is easier to fingerprint, and the site is not applying basic protections that modern web applications are expected to have. These weaknesses increase the likelihood of credential interception, session compromise, and user trust impact.

The most urgent corrective action is to enforce HTTPS for the application and all authentication flows, followed by deployment of core security headers such as Content-Security-Policy and Strict-Transport-Security.

\section*{Scope and Methodology}

This assessment was performed using passive analysis techniques only. The scan reviewed:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item HTTP response headers
    \item Page crawling and form discovery
    \item Form security analysis
    \item SSL/TLS availability checks
    \item Technology fingerprinting and version exposure review
\end{itemize}

No exploitation, payload delivery, or intrusive testing was performed. The report is based on the provided scan output and the observed application behavior.

