\section*{Business Impact Summary}

For business stakeholders, the main risks are:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item Credential exposure during login due to unencrypted transport
    \item Reduced user trust because the site lacks basic browser protections
    \item Easier targeting by attackers through visible software fingerprints
    \item Potential compliance concerns where HTTPS and header hardening are expected
\end{itemize}

The issues do not indicate a confirmed compromise, but they do indicate a weak security posture that should be corrected before the application is considered production-ready.

\section*{Recommended Remediation Plan}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.8em]
    \item \textbf{Immediately enforce HTTPS} for the entire application, including the login form and all sensitive endpoints.
    \item \textbf{Deploy core browser security headers}:
    \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
        \item Content-Security-Policy
        \item Strict-Transport-Security
        \item X-Frame-Options or frame-ancestors
        \item X-Content-Type-Options
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Reduce information disclosure} by hiding or normalizing Server and X-Powered-By headers.
    \item \textbf{Add privacy hardening headers} such as Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy.
    \item \textbf{Re-test the application} after changes to confirm transport security and header coverage.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Conclusion}

The assessed system currently presents a \textbf{Critical} security posture due to insecure authentication handling and missing baseline web protections. The most important fix is to eliminate HTTP usage for the application and its login flow. Once transport security is corrected, the next priority is to deploy browser hardening headers and reduce fingerprinting exposure.

\textbf{Final assessment:} The application should be remediated before further use in environments that handle sensitive data or user credentials.

